{#
settings/_sorting.html.twig
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What sorts this person's mail into tabs, and whether it outranks the provider.

Two independent questions, two forms, exactly like the compose-behaviour card
beside it: each control submits on change, and a shared form would post both
keys whenever either moved. The controller writes only the key it is sent, so
they cannot overwrite one another.

The same two-segment idiom every boolean on these pages wears.

Accepts:
  source            'rules' | 'assistant'
  overrideProvider  bool
  tabs              bool — whether the inbox is shown in tabs at all
  accountTabs       bool — whether an account's own inbox has them too (#55)
  assistantOffered  bool — whether categorisation is available to this person
                    at all. The choice is still SHOWN when it is not, because
                    hiding a setting is how somebody concludes the feature does
                    not exist; it is disabled and says why instead.
#}
{% import '_partials/_segmented.html.twig' as seg %}

<div class="px-4 py-3">

{# Whether there are tabs at all (#29), above what fills them: it is the first
   question, and the two controls below are about tabs somebody may not have.

   They are NOT disabled when this is off. Mail keeps being filed either way —
   that is what makes switching back instant — so what files it is still a
   real setting, and greying it out would say otherwise. A third form for the
   reason there were two: each control posts only its own key. #}
<form method="post" action="{{ path('app_settings_sorting_update') }}" class="mb-4 pb-4 border-b border-line" data-controller="ui--auto-submit">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('settings-sorting') }}">

    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
        <span class="min-w-0">
            <span class="block text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted">{{ 'settings.sorting.tabs.label'|trans }}</span>
            <span class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-0.5">{{ 'settings.sorting.tabs.hint'|trans }}</span>
        </span>

        {{ seg.control('tabs', [
            { value: '1', label: 'settings.sorting.tabs.on'|trans },
            { value: '0', label: 'settings.sorting.tabs.off'|trans },
        ], tabs ? '1' : '0', {
            label:  'settings.sorting.tabs.label'|trans,
            action: 'change->ui--auto-submit#submit',
        }) }}
    </div>

    {# The one combination worth interrupting somebody for. With the tabs off
       and the assistant still chosen, every new message costs a model call —
       and may be held back while it is made — to pick a tab nobody is shown.
       That is theirs to decide, so it is said and not prevented.

       Only where the assistant is actually doing it: chosen AND available. #}
    {% if not tabs and 'assistant' == source and assistantOffered %}
        <p class="mt-2 flex items-start gap-2 text-[11px] text-warning" role="note" data-sorting-tabs-warning>
            <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>{{ 'settings.sorting.tabs.assistant_warning'|trans }}</span>
        </p>
    {% endif %}
</form>

{# Whether an account's own inbox — the account row in the sidebar — has the
   tabs too (#55). Off unless switched on: it turns the page an account opens
   from its whole inbox into its Primary tab.

   Only while the tabs are on at all. With One list there are no tabs to show
   anywhere, and a switch that cannot do anything is a question with no
   answer; switching the tabs back on brings it back as it was left. Its own
   form, like the rest of this card, so it posts only its own key. #}
{% if tabs %}
<form method="post" action="{{ path('app_settings_sorting_update') }}" class="mb-4 pb-4 border-b border-line" data-controller="ui--auto-submit">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('settings-sorting') }}">

    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
        <span class="min-w-0">
            <span class="block text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted">{{ 'settings.sorting.account_tabs.label'|trans }}</span>
            <span class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-0.5">{{ 'settings.sorting.account_tabs.hint'|trans }}</span>
        </span>

        {{ seg.control('accountTabs', [
            { value: '1', label: 'settings.sorting.account_tabs.on'|trans },
            { value: '0', label: 'settings.sorting.account_tabs.off'|trans },
        ], accountTabs ? '1' : '0', {
            label:  'settings.sorting.account_tabs.label'|trans,
            action: 'change->ui--auto-submit#submit',
        }) }}
    </div>
</form>
{% endif %}

<form method="post" action="{{ path('app_settings_sorting_update') }}" data-controller="ui--auto-submit">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('settings-sorting') }}">

    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
        <span class="min-w-0">
            <span class="block text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted">{{ 'settings.sorting.source.label'|trans }}</span>
            <span class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-0.5">
                {{ ('settings.sorting.source.' ~ source ~ '_help')|trans }}
            </span>
        </span>

        {# The assistant option is refused rather than hidden when categorisation is
           not available: an administrator has to switch it on for the installation
           and the reader has to not have switched it off for themselves, and a
           control that vanished would leave somebody wondering which. #}
        {{ seg.control('source', [
            { value: 'rules',     label: 'settings.sorting.source.rules'|trans },
            { value: 'assistant', label: 'settings.sorting.source.assistant'|trans, disabled: not assistantOffered },
        ], source, {
            label:  'settings.sorting.source.label'|trans,
            action: 'change->ui--auto-submit#submit',
        }) }}
    </div>

    {% if not assistantOffered %}
        <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-2">{{ 'settings.sorting.source.unavailable'|trans }}</p>
    {% endif %}
</form>

<form method="post" action="{{ path('app_settings_sorting_update') }}" class="mt-4" data-controller="ui--auto-submit">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('settings-sorting') }}">

    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
        <span class="min-w-0">
            <span class="block text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted">{{ 'settings.sorting.override.label'|trans }}</span>
            <span class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-0.5">{{ 'settings.sorting.override.hint'|trans }}</span>
        </span>

        {{ seg.control('overrideProvider', [
            { value: '0', label: 'settings.sorting.override.provider'|trans },
            { value: '1', label: 'settings.sorting.override.plmail'|trans },
        ], overrideProvider ? '1' : '0', {
            label:  'settings.sorting.override.label'|trans,
            action: 'change->ui--auto-submit#submit',
        }) }}
    </div>
</form>

{# Asking again, which is a different thing from either control above.

   Those decide what sorts the mail; this re-asks the model about mail it has
   already answered for. It exists because the ANSWER goes stale while the mail
   does not: the prompt is editable, the model is a setting, and what plMail
   sends alongside a message has changed. Nothing re-asks on its own, because a
   model call per message is the most expensive thing here.

   Only where the assistant can actually be used. On a rules-only installation
   there is no verdict to refresh and the button would be an offer that does
   nothing. #}
{% if assistantOffered %}
    <form method="post" action="{{ path('app_settings_sorting_again') }}" class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-line">
        <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('settings-sorting') }}">

        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
            <span class="min-w-0">
                <span class="block text-xs font-medium text-ink-muted">{{ 'settings.sorting.again.label'|trans }}</span>
                <span class="block text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-0.5">{{ 'settings.sorting.again.hint'|trans }}</span>
            </span>

            <div class="flex items-center gap-1.5 shrink-0">
                {# A count and not a free number: the cost is model calls on a
                   host everybody shares, and three sizes is the whole of the
                   decision anybody actually has. #}
                <select name="limit"
                        class="h-8 rounded-pane border border-line bg-surface px-2 text-[11px] text-ink-muted cursor-pointer">
                    {% for size in [100, 200, 500] %}
                        <option value="{{ size }}" {{ 200 == size ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ size }}</option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </select>

                <button type="submit"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 h-8 px-3 rounded-pane border border-line
                               text-[11px] text-ink-muted hover:bg-hover cursor-pointer transition-colors">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-rotate text-[10px]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    {{ 'settings.sorting.again.button'|trans }}
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </form>
{% endif %}

{# Said once, under both, because it is true of both and it is the question
   anybody will have: what happens to the mail already in the tabs. It is
   re-filed, in the background, and a large mailbox takes a few minutes — which
   is worth saying, because a tab that has not changed yet otherwise reads as a
   setting that did nothing. #}
<p class="text-[11px] text-ink-faint mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-line">
    {{ 'settings.sorting.applies_to_new'|trans }}
</p>

</div>
